⚠️ **IMPORTANT: `openLoginPage()` moved outside the poll loop's `try/catch` — error surface changed**

In the base code the `openLoginPage()` call sat **inside** the `try { ... } on Object { /* keep polling */ }` that wrapped the whole probe iteration, so a throwing launch callback was swallowed and the poll spun on to the timeout. After the extraction the call lives here in the loop body, outside `_probeCodeMieSignIn`'s try/catch (which now only covers `probe()` and the JSON parse):

```dart
if (openLogin) {
  loginAsked = true;
  openLoginPage();   // used to be inside the swallow-everything try
}
```

A throwing `openLoginPage` now propagates out of `pollCodeMieSignIn` to its caller instead of being treated as transient. Arguably surfacing the failure is the better behavior — but it is a behavior change in a PR scoped as pure extraction (error-string/error-surface changes are behavior changes for callers and tests), and it happens precisely on the error path (a platform that cannot open the login tab), which is the path least covered by the happy-path test suites.

Please either restore the old swallow semantics (wrap the call in the same `on Object` guard with the "keep polling" intent made explicit) or call the change out in the PR description as an intentional tightening.

💡 **SUGGESTION: doc says "shared by forgetAll and dispose" — dispose still has its own inline copy**

`_cancelNoUiTimers` was extracted and its doc claims it is "shared by `[forgetAll]` and `[dispose]`", but `dispose()` still carries the identical inline loop a few lines below:

```dart
@override
void dispose() {
  _disposed = true;
  for (final timer in _noUiTimers.values) {
    timer.cancel();
  }
  _noUiTimers.clear();
  ...
}
```

Either replace the dispose copy with a `_cancelNoUiTimers()` call (the dedup the doc implies) or fix the doc. No behavior difference either way.

💡 **SUGGESTION: store/loader now constructed before the picker null-check**

The base flow returned early when the host cannot pick folders **before** constructing anything:

```dart
final folderPicker = picker ?? createFolderPicker();
if (folderPicker == null) return; // no-op
final store = loader?.appsStore ?? appsStore ?? AppsStore(manager.env);
final widgetLoader = loader ?? UnpackedWidgetLoader(env: manager.env, appsStore: store);
```

Now `loadUnpackedWidget` builds both objects up front and `_runUnpackedLoad` performs the early return after them. I verified both constructors are pure field stores, so this is inert today — but the evaluation order did technically change, and a future non-pure constructor would turn this early-return path into a side effect. Consider keeping the picker check first (e.g. passing a lazy `store ??= ...` resolution into the run stage), or at least note the ordering in the doc.

